// fs's coverage, and the argument for every point nothing reaches.
//
//   deno task coverage:fs
//   deno task coverage:fs --verbose
//
// **Named rather than tolerated as a percentage below 100.** A report that sits at 84.2% for ever
// teaches everyone to ignore the last line, and then a genuinely new gap arrives and looks like the one
// that was always there. So this is a two-way ratchet over `tools/wac/covledger.wac`: anything not
// listed here is expected to be covered and the run fails if it is not — and anything listed that *does*
// get covered fails too, since the reason has stopped holding. `issues/system/0222`.
//
// ## Pins and rules, and why this package needs both
//
// A pin names one line and proves it is still that line. That is right when the reason is about *that*
// line, and it is 33 of them here. But 94 of the 127 uncovered points are one fact repeated — an arm
// that needs a `Cli` only a built program has, or a peer only a real parent process can be — and 94
// pins would each carry the same sentence. A `Rule` states it once and says which points it covers.
//
// `scope` decides what the pattern is matched against: `"line"` the point's own, `"decl"` its enclosing
// declaration, `"struct"` its enclosing type. A rule that matches nothing fails, exactly as a moved pin
// does; that is what stops it being a blanket.
//
// ## `proven` is two different claims
//
//   - `true` — no input reaches it;
//   - `false` — reachable, and nobody has built the input here. **For most of this package that means
//     "driven by another test"**, which is a third thing again and is said in each `why`:
//     `test/host.test.ts` drives the host arms against the real filesystem and
//     `packages/box/test/sealing.test.ts` drives the remote ones through a real sealed session. This is
//     where the *measurement* stops, not where the testing does.
//
// ## `cov.ts` until 2026-08-20 — `issues/system/0161`
//
// The exercises were already wac: `test/wac/cov_probe.wac` has been the whole workload since the probe
// existed, and the TypeScript instrumented it, called its thirteen exports and held this ledger. The
// numbers are unchanged — 806 points, 679 covered — because the workload is unchanged.
//
// **Its failure message could not be reported, which is the thing this issue was filed about.**
// `tools/coverageAll.ts` repeats a failing task's own explanation and selects the lines by matching
// `no longer holds`, `is listed as unreach`, `branch point(s) uncovered` and a leading `error`. This
// package said "N uncovered branch point(s) are not accounted for" — the same words in the other order,
// matching none of them — so its *principal* failure exited 1 with nothing on screen but "(nothing
// matched the known failure shapes)". And that same phrase is how `coverageAll.ts` decides a package
// holds a coverage floor, so the strictest ledger here was counted among the ones that only check their
// own exemptions. The shared ratchet says it the one way.
//
// **And two of its 33 pins were the same entry twice, about a branch that is covered.** Both named
// `image.wac:339` — `if (r.bad) { return -1; }` — with the reason "one of the two `r.bad` checks inside
// the directory-entry loop … reachable, and not constructed". All five `r.bad` checks in that file are
// covered. So the ledger carried the same paragraph twice about a gap that was not one, and a staleness
// check could not tell the difference: line 336 still holds that text, so the snippet matched both
// times. The two-way ratchet fails it as listed-but-covered, which is how it was found. Both are gone
// and 31 pins remain.

import { Core, Cli } from "std/platform.wac";
import { Pin, Rule, Measured, measure, report, ratchet } from "../../../tools/wac/covledger.wac";

const string EXERCISE = "packages/fs/test/cov_exercise.wac";

/**
 * `src/` and **not** `packages/fs/`: the probe's own branches are a test's branches.
 *
 * Counting them would put the driver's coverage into the package's number — and, worse, into the
 * ratchet, where an unexercised line of the *probe* would read as an unaccounted line of the package.
 */
const string PREFIX = "packages/fs/src/";

