        "as far as reading bytes â so `/proc/9999/cmdline` (driven, in `procOps`) is " +
        "refused a step earlier. This is the `Proc?` unwrap the type system requires and it " +
        "has no second answer to give."));
}

/**
 * Classes of point, matched by a pattern rather than named one at a time.
 *
 * Every one of these is `proven: false` and every `why` says which *other* test drives it. That is the
 * shape of this package: what needs a capability or a peer is exercised by a built program against a
 * real filesystem, and a probe can build the filesystems that need neither and no more.
 */
Rule[] rules() {
  return Rule[](
    Rule("packages/fs/src/fs.wac", "case Host(cli)", "line", false,
         "A host mount, which takes a `Cli` that only a built program has. Every one of these " +
         "is driven by `test/host.test.ts` and " +
         "`packages/box/test/wac/backingsprocess_test.wac` against the real filesystem, which " +
         "is a better oracle than this probe could be â so this is where the measurement " +
         "stops, not where the testing does."),
    Rule("packages/fs/src/fs.wac", "case Remote(chan", "line", false,
         "A remote mount: the arm asks a *parent process* over 0116's channel. A probe cannot " +
         "be that peer â a wac fake has no state to answer from â so these are driven by " +
         "`packages/box/test/sealing.test.ts`, which runs a sealed session whose stages read, " +
         "write and rename through the channel. That claim was checked rather than remembered " +
         "on 2026-08-12: the file had no `mv` in it, so `remoteRename` was named here as " +
         "driven and driven by nothing. It has one now, canaried by making every rename " +
         "report success."),
    Rule("packages/fs/src/remote.wac", "struct Chan", "struct", false,
         "`Chan`'s own methods â `of`, `waitId`, `rearm`, `compact`. Each is about a live " +
         "handle: the id a `waitAny` waits on, the outstanding read that has to be re-armed, " +
         "the backlog that has to be compacted. There is no handle without a `Cli` and no " +
         "answer without a peer, so these move with the channel itself and are exercised " +
         "wherever a real child asks a real parent."),
    Rule("packages/fs/src/fs.wac", "Cli cli", "decl", false,
         "A constructor that takes a `Cli` â `onHost`, `overParent`, `overChan`, " +
         "`fromParentOrHost`. Only a built program has one, and what they build is exercised " +
         "by `test/host.test.ts` and by every spawned stage in " +
         "`packages/box/test/sealing.test.ts`; a probe can build the filesystems that need no " +
         "capability and no more."),
    Rule("packages/fs/src/fs.wac", "rename across mounts", "line", false,
         "The refusal `rename` gives when one end is on a *parent's* filesystem and the other " +
         "is not. Reaching it needs a mount with a `Remote` backing, which needs a channel to " +
         "a live peer â the same reason as the arms above, one level in: this is the `else` " +
         "inside `case Remote`, so a probe with no remote mount cannot get to either side of " +
         "it. `packages/box/test/sealing.test.ts` is where a stage renames across the " +
         "boundary."),
    Rule("packages/fs/src/wire.wac", "this.why == \"\"", "line", true,
         "**The `else` of first-error-wins, which cannot happen here.** Every reader that " +
         "records a reason sets `bad` in the same breath, and `byte()` answers zero for a " +
         "reader with `bad` set â so a reader that already has a `why` reads a length of " +
         "zero, zero is not negative, and this refusal is never reached a second time. The " +
         "guard is right and is what makes the property hold for the readers that *do* reach " +
         "it twice through other fields; here it is a statement rather than a branch. " +
         "`cov_probe.wac`'s `wireOps` drives the arm that does fire, and `fs_test.wac` " +
         "asserts the property from both sides. issues/lang/0112."),
    Rule("packages/fs/src/remote.wac", "remoteSetExecutable", "line", false,
         "**The one wire call with no driver anywhere**, named separately so the blanket " +
         "reason above does not cover for it. Every other opcode is reached by a sealed " +
         "session because some applet makes that call â `cat` reads, `cp` writes, `mv` " +
         "renames â but nothing in `packages/box` sets the executable bit: `chmod` is one " +
         "of the shell's *builtins*, so a sealed `chmod +x` never leaves the parent, and the " +
         "only caller of `setExecutable` in the repository is `packages/git`'s checkout, " +
         "which runs on a host filesystem. So this is not 'driven elsewhere' â it is " +
         "untested, and it is written down here rather than inside a category that says " +
         "otherwise. A driver would mean an applet that sets the bit â and `packages/sh`'s " +
         "README says why there is not one: `chmod` is a *builtin*, along with `echo`, " +
         "`test`, `ls` and `chown`, because 'a builtin is what the shell must answer itself'. " +
         "So this is not an oversight waiting for a small change; driving it means arguing " +
         "with that decision, and the cheaper honest answer is this entry."),
    Rule("packages/fs/src/remote.wac", "Chan c", "decl", false,
         "The child's half of the channel: every one of these writes a question and waits for " +
         "its parent's answer. The *wire* underneath them is covered â `fs_test.wac` " +
         "round-trips all four encode/decode pairs â and the asking needs a real peer, " +
         "which is `packages/box/test/sealing.test.ts`."));
}

export i32 main(Core core, Cli cli) {
  bool verbose = false;
  i32 argc = cli.argCount().wait();
  for (i32 i = 0; i < argc; i++) {
    if (string.fromBytes(cli.arg(i).wait()) == "--verbose") { verbose = true; }
  }

  // No grants: every filesystem the probe builds needs no capability, which is what makes it a probe.
  Measured m = measure(core, cli, EXERCISE, string[0](fill: ""), string[0](fill: ""));
  if (m.nPoints == 0) { return 1; }

  report(core, m.points, m.nPoints, m.counts, PREFIX, verbose);
  return ratchet(core, cli, m.points, m.nPoints, m.counts, PREFIX, pins(), rules()) ? 1 : 0;
}
